# What stackroom writes. `stackroom build` rewrites its output folder on every
# run and leaves a `.stackroom` marker in it; none of it belongs in git.
/site/
.stackroom

# Scratch collections used while developing. Documents under here are somebody
# else's material and frequently not ours to publish - keep them out of the
# repository, not merely out of the release.
/demo/

# A safety rail, not a rule about file types. The test suite manufactures the
# PDFs it needs (tests/synth.py) precisely so that no document has to be checked
# in, and a PDF that arrives in this repository by accident is somebody's
# release - possibly one that was never meant to be public, and git does not
# forget. If you have a genuine reason to commit one: `git add -f <path>`.
*.pdf

# Editors
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.#*
\#*#

# Operating systems
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored
#
# src/stackroom/assets/fonts/*.woff2 are committed on purpose. Guarantee 5 in
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md is that a published archive loads nothing from a
# third-party host, which means the fonts ship in the wheel, which means they
# live in the repository. They are built by tools/build-fonts.sh, they are
# reproducible from it, and they are small - but do not add a *.woff2 rule
# here: an archive that fetches its typefaces from a font service is a log of
# who read what.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
